.TH diff3 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
diff3 \- compare three files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B diff3
[\fB\-ex3\fR] \fIfile1 file2 file3\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B diff3
compares three versions of a file and reports the ranges that differ,
which is what you want when two people have both changed a common
ancestor.
.PP
Each difference is flagged with which files disagree:
.TP
.B ====
All three are different.
.TP
.B ====1
.I file1
is the odd one out.
.TP
.B ====2
.I file2
is.
.TP
.B ====3
.I file3
is.
.PP
Under each flag come the ranges from each file, in
.BR ed (1)
notation, and the text.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-e
Write an
.BR ed (1)
script that applies to
.I file1
the changes between
.I file2
and
.IR file3 .
This is the merge: pipe it into
.B ed
with a
.B w
and a
.B q
after it.
.TP
.B \-x
Like
.BR \-e ,
but only for the parts where all three differ.
.TP
.B \-3
Like
.BR \-e ,
but only for the parts where
.I file3
is the odd one out.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# diff3 mine.c base.c theirs.c
# ( diff3 -e mine.c base.c theirs.c ; echo w ; echo q ) | ed - mine.c
.fi
.SH NOTES
.B diff3
calls
.BR diff (1)
twice, so it needs the memory for that on top of its own.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR diff (1),
.BR diffh (1),
.BR ed (1),
.BR cmp (1)
